WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.740
Hi, I'm Chuck Blake. I'm based in Brooklyn, and I've spent my whole career across business,

00:03.860 --> 00:08.260
product, engineering, marketing, operations, all sorts of roles, all at early stage companies.

00:08.700 --> 00:12.380
I'm technical, and I've always been interested in what technology can do to help grow businesses.

00:12.600 --> 00:16.840
And I like messy workflows, business problems. I like figuring out where the real constraints are

00:16.840 --> 00:21.660
and in building something useful around that. I like to take what's working in a system and

00:21.660 --> 00:25.280
make it repeatable so I can run it over and over again. And I've done this from a lot of

00:25.280 --> 00:31.300
different perspectives. I was an operations leader at an early stage startup building AI

00:31.300 --> 00:35.960
infrastructure. I was a technical co-founder. Most recently, I was CTO at a company called

00:35.960 --> 00:39.900
LEA and co-founder, and we were building AI document intelligence for wealth management.

00:40.420 --> 00:45.360
Today, I am doing some work as a fractional CTO at a healthcare startup, and I also build a lot of my

00:45.360 --> 00:50.300
own side projects, including Glance, which we're talking about here today. And I also run a website

00:50.300 --> 00:55.060
that helps independent musicians promote their music. I'm a tinkerer by default, and I've spent

00:55.060 --> 01:01.100
a lot of my time building my tooling, like coding workflows, my agent skills, monitors, reporting

01:01.100 --> 01:06.060
systems. And I really like to treat all that tooling as part of the product. And the interesting

01:06.060 --> 01:09.960
thing, I think, about working with AI tools these days is that a lot of that tinkering really pays

01:09.960 --> 01:14.840
off right away, whereas in the past, it felt like a lot of building reports around your to-do system

01:14.840 --> 01:18.520
or building a knowledge management system. And a lot of times, it didn't have the payoff,

01:18.940 --> 01:22.280
and it felt like you were spending a lot of time tinkering. Now, I feel like that tinkering

01:22.280 --> 01:27.360
really is like you almost see results like right away. And I think that's like very exciting.

01:28.000 --> 01:33.300
And again, like this moment now, especially this year, 2026 has been crazy. I think it's like a

01:33.300 --> 01:38.280
really interesting time for somebody like me. You know, AI gives my mix of business judgment and

01:38.280 --> 01:43.500
technical experience somewhere to play. And I have enough leverage to test like a lot of different

01:43.500 --> 01:48.520
ways of building companies and building products. And that's been a lot of fun. And I like staying

01:48.520 --> 01:53.120
close to the problem. And that's why I like kind of working on a lot of these side projects

01:53.120 --> 01:57.800
and where I can really get in with customers and seeing what their problems are and working with

01:57.800 --> 01:58.300
them.
